                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1782-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_sc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Supercardioid" title="Supercardioid" ></span>Electro-Voice ND46</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Supercardioid Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The EV ND46 is an end-address, supercardioid dynamic microphone. It is an updated version of the N/D 468. It is part of EV&#8217;s new ND line, which replaces the N/Dyn microphones. The ND46 is designed to be used on drums, guitar cabinets, and bass cabinets.</p><p>The head of the ND46 is yoke-mounted with a swiveling head, and has a unique locking feature. Depress the lock, and the head swivels freely. Once the lock is released, the head stays in place. This prevents the microphone&#8217;s element from changing position if the head is bumped (or hit with a stick), while making initial positioning easy.</p><p>The yoke is made from die-cast zinc, with a die-cast aluminum shank, and, like its siblings in the ND line, a &#8220;memraflex&#8221; grill screen that is designed to withstand rough treatment (i.e. drum stick hits and drops) without denting.</p><p>Also like the other ND mics, the ND46 has a large diaphragm moving-coil cartridge with a Mylar diaphragm. According to Electro-Voice, the lightweight diaphragm has low mass for quick transient response. The diaphragm has a cloth layer over it for moisture (i.e. sweat) protection. The magnet, as the &#8220;ND&#8221; would indicate, is neodymium, giving the microphone a high output with lighter physical weight. The capsule is fitted with a humbucking coil to guard against EMI (electromagnetic interference).</p><p>Like its little brother the ND44, the ND46 does not have a great deal of low frequency response when positioned at a distance from the source. However, it is cleverly designed so as to take advantage of proximity effect. When placed at 2.5cm from the source, there is a low end boost of 10db at 100Hz. The engineer is therefore encouraged to vary the distance from the source to tailor the low end response to his or her liking. </p><p>The ND46 is sold with a mic clip and a cloth bag.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Electro-Voice/ND46">Electro-Voice ND46</a> is also known as: ND 46, ND-46.</p><p>The mic was released in 2016.</p>
